// arena.mn — the arena: `(body) ~> arena`.
//
// An arena's allocations die at its exit except what its publication
// reaches: the body's value, and what the body stored into memory older than
// the arena. What survives moves out of the arena's region and the rest is
// reclaimed in one step, the memory zero again behind it — the virginity
// every reset keeps (lib/memory.mn's `heap_reset`).
//
// The arena HANDLES THE BODY'S ALLOCATION: it answers `Alloc`, so the
// projection roster (src/effects.mn) classes its install as an arena and the
// lowering replaces the install with the arena itself — no record, no world
// push, and these arms never run. The body's allocations still bump the one
// heap line; the arena is where on that line they begin.
//
// What the body stored into older memory is JOURNALED where the store
// happens, with the type the store already knows. Whether a store can reach
// older memory is read off its target's edges — a buffer the scope allocated
// cannot, a parameter is its callers' question, a handler's state its
// installs' — and a list slot's store, a state commit or a `<~` tick that can
// reads its value as a move, so the body is specialized by what carrying
// that value out of a region needs. At the exit a journaled slot that holds
// a value of the region is a root, moved through its own type; a value whose
// type its store could not see — a function, a continuation — keeps the
// whole region, nothing moved, and the census says so, because moving a
// value whose type is unknown is the one thing the arena never does. The
// exit asks no one: nothing the program wrote has to name a buffer.
handler arena {
  alloc(n) => resume(alloc(n)),
  heap_mark() => resume(heap_mark()),
  heap_reset(m) => resume(heap_reset(m)),
}

// The fact that code opens an arena, charged to the frame the install stands
// in and answered by no handler — so `main`'s row says whether the program
// opens one, which is what turns every store's barrier on.
effect Arena {
  
}
